# Netlify rewrite rules — not a redirect to a new URL, a rewrite that
# serves different content while keeping the URL the visitor sees.
#
# /templates renders content built to templates-page/index.html rather
# than templates/index.html, because templates/ is reserved for the
# site's page-template sources (raw files containing {{tokens}}, not
# meant to be a real page) and already sits at the repo root alongside
# every other output directory.
/templates              /templates-page/index.html   200
/templates/              /templates-page/index.html   200

# Everything else under /templates/* (templates/home.html, etc.) is a raw
# page-template source — {{tokens}} unresolved, never meant to be public.
# It was reachable because Netlify's pretty-URL resolution matches
# /templates/home straight to the literal file templates/home.html, which
# sits in the published root like every other file.
#
# The first attempt at this rule (no trailing !) did not work — verified
# against real edge-request logs from the deployed review site, not just
# a local render: /templates/home kept returning 200 with raw {{tokens}}
# after that fix shipped. Root cause: by default, when a redirect rule's
# path matches an existing static file, Netlify serves that file directly
# and skips the redirect entirely — the rule only "wins" over a real file
# with the explicit force flag (!) after the status code. templates/home.html
# is a real file sitting in the published root, so the unforced rule was
# always a no-op. The two exact-match rules above still take priority for
# the real Templates shop page (templates-page/index.html, a different
# directory, not a raw source).
/templates/*             /index.html                  404!
